preventionRule:
Never use 'catch [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException]' as the handler in WinForms event handlers or any scriptblock invoked as a .NET delegate. Use a bare 'catch {}' with an explicit type check:

  catch {
    if ($_ -is [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException] -or
        ($null -ne $_.Exception -and $_.Exception -is [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException])) {
      # stop timer + exit message pump
      try { $timer.Stop() } catch {}
      try { [System.Windows.Forms.Application]::ExitThread() } catch {}
      return
    }
    Write-Warning "Handler error: $_"
  }

remedy:
Replace typed catch:
  BEFORE: } catch [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException] { return }
  AFTER:  } catch { if ($_ -is [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException] -or ($null -ne $_.Exception -and $_.Exception -is [System.Management.Automation.PipelineStoppedException])) { try { $timer.Stop() } catch {}; try { [System.Windows.Forms.Application]::ExitThread() } catch {}; return }; Write-Warning "Handler error: $_" }
